In Linux, everything starts from / (root directory).

Think of / as the main folder of the entire Linux system.

/
├── bin
├── boot
├── dev
├── etc
├── home
├── lib
├── opt
├── root
├── tmp
├── usr
└── var

1. / — Root

This is the top/main directory.

cd /
ls

All other directories are located under /.

2. /home — Normal users' personal files

Each normal user usually gets a directory here.

Example:

/home/bilal
/home/user1
/home/user2

Your documents, scripts, downloads, etc. can be stored here.

cd /home
ls

3. /root — Root user's home

root is the administrator account.

The root user's personal home directory is:

/root

⚠️ Don't confuse:

/       → Root of the entire filesystem
/root   → Home directory of root user

4. /etc — Configuration files ⭐

This is very important for a Linux administrator.

It contains system and application configuration files.

Examples:

/etc/ssh/
/etc/hosts
/etc/passwd
/etc/fstab

For example:

cat /etc/hosts

5. /var — Frequently changing data

var means variable.

It contains data that changes frequently, such as:

Logs
Cache
Application data

For example:

/var/log/

As a Linux administrator, you'll frequently work with:

cd /var/log
ls

6. /tmp — Temporary files

Used for temporary data.

cd /tmp

Applications and users may create temporary files here.

7. /boot — Boot-related files

Contains files required during the Linux boot process.

For example, you may see:

/boot/vmlinuz...
/boot/initrd...

The Linux kernel is also stored here.

8. /dev — Devices

dev means devices.

Linux represents many hardware devices as files inside /dev.

Examples:

/dev/sda
/dev/nvme0n1
/dev/null

For example:

ls /dev

9. /usr — User programs and files

Contains many programs, libraries and other files used by the system.

For example:

/usr/bin
/usr/lib
/usr/share

You can think of /usr as a major location for installed software and supporting files.

10. /bin — Essential commands

Contains essential commands used by users and the system.

Examples include commands such as:

ls
cp
mv
cat

On modern Linux systems, /bin is often a symbolic link to /usr/bin.

11. /sbin — System administration commands

Contains commands mainly used for system administration.

Examples include tools related to:

Disk management
Networking
System configuration

Like /bin, on many modern distributions /sbin is also linked into /usr.

12. /opt — Optional software

Used for additional/third-party software.

For example, an organization might install some application under:

/opt/application/
13. /lib — Libraries

Contains important libraries required by programs and the system.

On modern distributions, /lib may also be a symbolic link into /usr/lib.
